  "articleSummary": "The article argues that AI-enabled cyber threats will grow through two compounding capabilities—longer autonomous task execution and broader coordination of sub-agents—rather than a single dramatic breakthrough. Citing METR’s task-horizon research and a reported Claude Code–orchestrated espionage campaign, it frames capability as duration × breadth, then explains how “LLMjacking” lets attackers use stolen cloud credentials while victims pay the compute bill. Cheap, repeated attempts can overwhelm human-paced defenses even when individual success rates are low, undermining reassurance based on pricing gates or per-attempt failure rates. A pointed, darkly humorous security analysis aimed at security teams and technical decision-makers skeptical of agentic threats, it repeatedly uses Terminator/Skynet imagery, swarms, and billing metaphors to recast relentless machine endurance as relentless, victim-funded computation.",
